    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Being able to “read” another person’s face is one of the most vital parts of human interpersonal connection. However, as most biological functions are, facial emotional processing is not immune to dysfunction. Past research has shown that in populations that struggle with sleep disturbance or depressive symptoms, facial emotion processing capabilities become impaired in both reaction time and accuracy to varying degrees. In this study, we aim to investigate whether behavioral therapy for sleep, or Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I), could restore facial emotion processing functions in a population that struggles with both insomnia and depressive symptoms. After data analysis, the study found that in terms of accuracy, the emotion categories of sad and surprise increased in accurate responses post-treatment as compared to pre-treatment; in terms of reaction time, the results showed a significant improvement in response time in the emotion categories of anger, fear, and happy. The results of this study imply that there is a possible correlation between CBT-I and improvement in facial emotion recognition in both accuracy and time, but only for specific emotion categories.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Gong, N. and Goldstein-Piekarski, A. (2024). Investigating the Impact of Sleep Therapy on Facial Emotion Processing in Populations with Mood Disturbance. Version 1. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hv612rx4891/version/1.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
